// Shared Syncthing ignore patterns applied to every folder by apply-ignores.sh.
// Keep this list generic — no project, path, or host-specific entries.
// Per-folder overrides live in ~/.config/syncthing-sync/overrides.json (not versioned).

// macOS
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.fseventsd
.TemporaryItems
.DocumentRevisions-V100
Icon?

// Editors / IDEs
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea
.vscode/.history

// Build / dependency directories
// Only patterns that are unambiguously tool-specific go here. Common English
// words like `build`, `dist`, `target` are intentionally NOT included — they
// can collide with user data directories named the same thing (e.g. a CNC
// project's `build/` containing photos). Add those as per-folder overrides
// in ~/.config/syncthing-sync/overrides.json for code-only folders.
node_modules
.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc
.next
.turbo
.cache

// VCS junk
//
// Goal: replicate enough of .git/ that branches/refs/objects flow between
// machines, while suppressing the in-flight files that cause sync-conflict
// noise and (worse) stranded loose objects.
//
// Fully synced: refs, packed objects, completed loose objects, config.
// Excluded:    lock files, atomic-rename temp files, OS metadata.
//
// This does NOT prevent legitimate conflicts when both machines write the
// same ref (e.g. both committing on `main` independently). For that you
// need single-writer discipline; see resolve-git-sync-conflicts for repair.
.git/*.lock
.git/**/*.lock
.git/objects/*/tmp_*
.git/objects/pack/tmp_*
